// Rep Max questions: Maths (AQA 7357). One question per line, no commas between lines.
// Fields: id (unique), s (subject), t (topic id from data/spec.js), lv (1 warm-up, 2 working set, 3 one-rep max),
// q (question; maths between $ signs, backslashes doubled), code (optional), o (four options), a (index 0-3 of the right one), x (explanation).
// A line that isn't valid is skipped and listed at the bottom of the home screen. Lines starting // are ignored.
{"id":"ma-001","s":"maths","t":"p1-01","lv":1,"q":"Simplify $\\frac{(2x^3)^4}{8x^5}$.","o":["$2x^7$","$16x^7$","$2x^{17}$","$8x^7$"],"a":0,"x":"$(2x^3)^4 = 16x^{12}$ (raise the 2 to the power too). Then $16\\div 8 = 2$ and $x^{12}\\div x^5 = x^7$."}
{"id":"ma-002","s":"maths","t":"p1-01","lv":2,"q":"Write $\\frac{6}{3-\\sqrt{3}}$ in the form $a+\\sqrt{b}$.","o":["$3-\\sqrt{3}$","$3+\\sqrt{3}$","$\\frac{3+\\sqrt{3}}{2}$","$6+2\\sqrt{3}$"],"a":1,"x":"Multiply top and bottom by $3+\\sqrt3$. The denominator becomes $9-3=6$, so you get $\\frac{6(3+\\sqrt3)}{6}=3+\\sqrt3$."}
{"id":"ma-003","s":"maths","t":"p1-01","lv":2,"q":"Write $\\frac{3+\\sqrt{5}}{3-\\sqrt{5}}$ in the form $a+b\\sqrt{5}$, where $a$ and $b$ are rational.","o":["$1+\\frac{3}{7}\\sqrt{5}$","$\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{5}$","$\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{5}$","$7+3\\sqrt{5}$"],"a":2,"x":"Multiply by $\\frac{3+\\sqrt5}{3+\\sqrt5}$: numerator $14+6\\sqrt5$, denominator $9-5=4$. So $\\frac{14+6\\sqrt5}{4}=\\frac72+\\frac32\\sqrt5$."}
{"id":"ma-004","s":"maths","t":"p1-02","lv":1,"q":"Write $x^2-6x+1$ in the form $(x+p)^2+q$.","o":["$(x-3)^2-8$","$(x-3)^2+10$","$(x+3)^2-8$","$(x-6)^2-35$"],"a":0,"x":"Halve the $x$ coefficient: $(x-3)^2 = x^2-6x+9$. To get back to $+1$ you must subtract 8, giving $(x-3)^2-8$."}
{"id":"ma-005","s":"maths","t":"p1-02","lv":2,"q":"The equation $x^2+kx+9=0$ has equal roots. Which are the possible values of $k$?","o":["$k=6$ only","$k=\\pm 3$","$k=\\pm 6$","$k=\\pm 36$"],"a":2,"x":"Equal roots means $b^2-4ac=0$, so $k^2-36=0$. Then $k^2=36$ and $k=6$ or $k=-6$. Do not forget the negative root."}
{"id":"ma-006","s":"maths","t":"p1-02","lv":3,"q":"The minimum value of $2x^2-12x+7$ is $m$, reached when $x=n$. What are $m$ and $n$?","o":["$m=-11,\\ n=-3$","$m=-11,\\ n=3$","$m=-4,\\ n=3$","$m=-18,\\ n=3$"],"a":1,"x":"Complete the square: $2(x^2-6x)+7=2[(x-3)^2-9]+7=2(x-3)^2-11$. Minimum is $-11$ at $x=3$. The $-9$ must be multiplied by 2."}
{"id":"ma-007","s":"maths","t":"p1-03","lv":1,"q":"Solve $x^2-x-12>0$.","o":["$-3<x<4$","$x<-4$ or $x>3$","$x<-3$ or $x>4$","$-4<x<3$"],"a":2,"x":"Factorise: $(x-4)(x+3)>0$. The graph is a U-shape, positive outside the roots $-3$ and $4$, so $x<-3$ or $x>4$."}
{"id":"ma-008","s":"maths","t":"p1-03","lv":2,"q":"Solve simultaneously $y=x+1$ and $x^2+y^2=25$. Which are the solutions $(x,y)$?","o":["$(3,4)$ and $(-4,-3)$","$(-3,-2)$ and $(4,5)$","$(3,2)$ and $(-4,-5)$","$(3,4)$ and $(-4,-5)$"],"a":0,"x":"Substitute: $x^2+(x+1)^2=25$ gives $x^2+x-12=0$, so $x=3$ or $-4$. Then use $y=x+1$ to get $y=4$ and $y=-3$."}
{"id":"ma-009","s":"maths","t":"p1-03","lv":2,"q":"Find all $x$ satisfying both $x^2-2x-8<0$ and $3x+1>2$.","o":["$\\frac13<x<4$","$-2<x<4$","$x>\\frac13$","$-2<x<\\frac13$"],"a":0,"x":"The quadratic gives $(x-4)(x+2)<0$, so $-2<x<4$. The linear one gives $x>\\frac13$. Both must hold, so take the overlap: $\\frac13<x<4$."}
{"id":"ma-010","s":"maths","t":"p1-04","lv":1,"q":"The graph of $y=f(x)$ is transformed to $y=f(x+3)$. Which describes the transformation?","o":["Translation by $\\begin{pmatrix}3\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}$","Translation by $\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\3\\end{pmatrix}$","Stretch scale factor 3 in the $x$-direction","Translation by $\\begin{pmatrix}-3\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}$"],"a":3,"x":"Replacing $x$ by $x+3$ moves the graph 3 units to the left, which is the translation $\\begin{pmatrix}-3\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}$. Changes inside the brackets act 'backwards'."}
{"id":"ma-011","s":"maths","t":"p1-04","lv":2,"q":"The curve $y=f(x)$ has a minimum at $(2,-5)$. Where is the minimum of $y=2f(x-1)+3$?","o":["$(3,-7)$","$(1,-7)$","$(3,-13)$","$(3,-4)$"],"a":0,"x":"$x-1$ shifts right 1: $x=3$. The $y$-value $-5$ is doubled to $-10$ first, then $+3$ gives $-7$. Order matters: stretch before translating."}
{"id":"ma-012","s":"maths","t":"p1-05","lv":1,"q":"Which is the equation of the line through $(2,3)$ with gradient $-2$?","o":["$2x+y+1=0$","$2x-y-1=0$","$x-2y+4=0$","$2x+y-7=0$"],"a":3,"x":"$y-3=-2(x-2)$ gives $y=-2x+7$, so $2x+y-7=0$. Check: $2(2)+3-7=0$."}
{"id":"ma-013","s":"maths","t":"p1-05","lv":3,"q":"The line through $(1,5)$ perpendicular to $3x+2y=12$ meets the axes at $A$ (on the $x$-axis) and $B$ (on the $y$-axis). Find the area of triangle $OAB$.","o":["$\\frac{169}{12}$","$\\frac{169}{6}$","$\\frac{49}{12}$","$\\frac{169}{4}$"],"a":0,"x":"Gradient of $3x+2y=12$ is $-\\frac32$, so the perpendicular has gradient $\\frac23$: $2x-3y+13=0$. Intercepts $-\\frac{13}{2}$ and $\\frac{13}{3}$. Area $=\\frac12\\times\\frac{13}{2}\\times\\frac{13}{3}=\\frac{169}{12}$."}
{"id":"ma-014","s":"maths","t":"p1-06","lv":1,"q":"What are the centre and radius of the circle $x^2+y^2-6x+4y-12=0$?","o":["Centre $(-3,2)$, radius $5$","Centre $(3,-2)$, radius $5$","Centre $(3,-2)$, radius $25$","Centre $(3,-2)$, radius $\\sqrt{13}$"],"a":1,"x":"Complete the square: $(x-3)^2+(y+2)^2=12+9+4=25$. Centre $(3,-2)$ and radius $\\sqrt{25}=5$. Remember to square-root the 25."}
{"id":"ma-015","s":"maths","t":"p1-06","lv":2,"q":"Circle $C$ has equation $(x-2)^2+(y+1)^2=20$. Find the tangent to $C$ at the point $(4,3)$.","o":["$x-2y+2=0$","$2x+y-11=0$","$2x-y-5=0$","$x+2y-10=0$"],"a":3,"x":"Centre $(2,-1)$, so radius gradient $=\\frac{3+1}{4-2}=2$. Tangent gradient is $-\\frac12$: $y-3=-\\frac12(x-4)$, giving $x+2y-10=0$."}
{"id":"ma-016","s":"maths","t":"p1-06","lv":3,"q":"The line $y=x+k$ is a tangent to the circle $x^2+y^2=8$. Which are the possible values of $k$?","o":["$k=\\pm2\\sqrt2$","$k=4$ only","$k=\\pm8$","$k=\\pm4$"],"a":3,"x":"Substitute: $2x^2+2kx+k^2-8=0$. A tangent means one root, so discriminant $=0$: $4k^2-8(k^2-8)=0$, giving $k^2=16$ and $k=\\pm4$."}
{"id":"ma-017","s":"maths","t":"p1-07","lv":1,"q":"Which pair of numbers is a counterexample to the claim 'the sum of two irrational numbers is irrational'?","o":["$\\sqrt2$ and $\\sqrt3$","$\\sqrt2$ and $-\\sqrt2$","$\\pi$ and $\\pi$","$\\sqrt4$ and $\\sqrt9$"],"a":1,"x":"$\\sqrt2+(-\\sqrt2)=0$, which is rational, so both numbers are irrational but the sum is not. $\\sqrt4=2$ and $\\sqrt9=3$ are rational, so they do not count."}
{"id":"ma-018","s":"maths","t":"p1-07","lv":2,"q":"$(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)=x^3+kx^2-3x+4$. Find $k$.","o":["$k=6$","$k=-2$","$k=2$","$k=-6$"],"a":3,"x":"Factor theorem: $f(-1)=0$. So $-1+k+3+4=0$, giving $k+6=0$ and $k=-6$. Use $x=-1$, not $x=1$."}
{"id":"ma-019","s":"maths","t":"p1-07","lv":2,"q":"Given that $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x)=2x^3-3x^2-11x+6$, fully factorise $f(x)$.","o":["$(x-3)(2x+1)(x-2)$","$(x-3)(2x-1)(x-2)$","$(x-3)(2x-1)(x+2)$","$(x+3)(2x-1)(x+2)$"],"a":2,"x":"Dividing by $(x-3)$ gives $2x^2+3x-2$, which factorises as $(2x-1)(x+2)$. Check $f(3)=54-27-33+6=0$."}
{"id":"ma-020","s":"maths","t":"p1-08","lv":1,"q":"Find the coefficient of $x^2$ in the expansion of $(1+2x)^5$.","o":["$20$","$10$","$80$","$40$"],"a":3,"x":"$\\binom52(2x)^2=10\\times4x^2=40x^2$. Remember to square the 2 as well as the $x$."}
{"id":"ma-021","s":"maths","t":"p1-08","lv":2,"q":"Find the coefficient of $x^3$ in the expansion of $(3-x)^6$.","o":["$540$","$-180$","$-540$","$-1620$"],"a":2,"x":"$\\binom63\\,3^3(-x)^3=20\\times27\\times(-1)x^3=-540x^3$. The odd power of $-x$ makes it negative."}
{"id":"ma-022","s":"maths","t":"p1-08","lv":2,"q":"Find the coefficient of $x^2$ in the expansion of $(1+x)(2+x)^5$.","o":["$80$","$120$","$240$","$160$"],"a":3,"x":"$(2+x)^5=32+80x+80x^2+\\dots$. The $x^2$ term of the product is $1\\times80x^2+x\\times80x=160x^2$."}
{"id":"ma-023","s":"maths","t":"p1-09","lv":1,"q":"In triangle $ABC$, $AB=5$ cm, $AC=8$ cm and angle $A=60^\\circ$. Find $BC$.","o":["$7$ cm","$49$ cm","$\\sqrt{129}$ cm","$\\sqrt{89}$ cm"],"a":0,"x":"Cosine rule: $BC^2=25+64-2(5)(8)\\cos60^\\circ=89-40=49$, so $BC=7$. Don't stop at 49, and remember the minus sign."}
{"id":"ma-024","s":"maths","t":"p1-09","lv":2,"q":"A triangle has sides $5$, $7$ and $8$ cm. Find its exact area.","o":["$20\\sqrt3$ cm²","$5\\sqrt3$ cm²","$10\\sqrt3$ cm²","$10$ cm²"],"a":2,"x":"Angle opposite 7: $\\cos\\theta=\\frac{25+64-49}{80}=\\frac12$, so $60^\\circ$. Area $=\\frac12(5)(8)\\sin60^\\circ=20\\times\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}=10\\sqrt3$."}
{"id":"ma-025","s":"maths","t":"p1-10","lv":1,"q":"Simplify $\\frac{1-\\cos^2x}{\\sin x}$.","o":["$\\cos x$","$\\tan x$","$\\sin x$","$\\frac{1}{\\sin x}$"],"a":2,"x":"Since $\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x=1$, we have $1-\\cos^2x=\\sin^2x$. Then $\\frac{\\sin^2x}{\\sin x}=\\sin x$."}
{"id":"ma-026","s":"maths","t":"p1-10","lv":2,"q":"Solve $2\\sin^2x-\\sin x-1=0$ for $0^\\circ\\le x<360^\\circ$.","o":["$210^\\circ,\\ 330^\\circ$","$30^\\circ,\\ 90^\\circ,\\ 150^\\circ$","$90^\\circ,\\ 210^\\circ,\\ 330^\\circ$","$90^\\circ,\\ 150^\\circ,\\ 210^\\circ,\\ 330^\\circ$"],"a":2,"x":"Factorise: $(2\\sin x+1)(\\sin x-1)=0$. $\\sin x=1$ gives $90^\\circ$. $\\sin x=-\\frac12$ gives $210^\\circ$ and $330^\\circ$ (third and fourth quadrants)."}
{"id":"ma-027","s":"maths","t":"p1-10","lv":3,"q":"Solve $\\tan\\theta=3\\sin\\theta$ for $0^\\circ\\le\\theta<360^\\circ$. Give non-exact answers to 1 d.p.","o":["$0^\\circ,\\ 70.5^\\circ,\\ 180^\\circ,\\ 289.5^\\circ$","$70.5^\\circ,\\ 289.5^\\circ$","$0^\\circ,\\ 109.5^\\circ,\\ 180^\\circ,\\ 250.5^\\circ$","$0^\\circ,\\ 70.5^\\circ,\\ 289.5^\\circ$"],"a":0,"x":"$\\frac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}=3\\sin\\theta$, so $\\sin\\theta(1-3\\cos\\theta)=0$. Never divide by $\\sin\\theta$. $\\sin\\theta=0$: $0^\\circ,180^\\circ$. $\\cos\\theta=\\frac13$: $70.5^\\circ,289.5^\\circ$."}
{"id":"ma-028","s":"maths","t":"p1-11","lv":1,"q":"Find the magnitude of the vector $5\\mathbf{i}-12\\mathbf{j}$.","o":["$17$","$13$","$7$","$\\sqrt{119}$"],"a":1,"x":"$|5\\mathbf{i}-12\\mathbf{j}|=\\sqrt{5^2+(-12)^2}=\\sqrt{169}=13$. Square both components, then add."}
{"id":"ma-029","s":"maths","t":"p1-11","lv":2,"q":"$\\overrightarrow{OA}=2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\overrightarrow{OB}=6\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}$. $M$ is the midpoint of $AB$. Find $|\\overrightarrow{OM}|$.","o":["$2\\sqrt{17}$","$\\sqrt{17}$","$2\\sqrt{2}$","$4\\sqrt{2}$"],"a":1,"x":"$\\overrightarrow{OM}=\\frac12(\\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{OB})=4\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}$. Its magnitude is $\\sqrt{16+1}=\\sqrt{17}$."}
{"id":"ma-030","s":"maths","t":"p1-12","lv":1,"q":"Differentiating $f(x)=x^2$ from first principles, $\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}$ simplifies to which expression before letting $h\\to0$?","o":["$2x$","$2x+h$","$x+h$","$2x+h^2$"],"a":1,"x":"$\\frac{(x+h)^2-x^2}{h}=\\frac{2xh+h^2}{h}=2x+h$. As $h\\to0$ this tends to $2x$."}
{"id":"ma-031","s":"maths","t":"p1-12","lv":2,"q":"Find the equation of the tangent to $y=x^3-3x+2$ at the point where $x=2$.","o":["$y=9x+4$","$y=3x-2$","$y=9x-14$","$y=-\\frac19x+\\frac{38}{9}$"],"a":2,"x":"At $x=2$, $y=4$. $\\frac{dy}{dx}=3x^2-3=9$. Tangent: $y-4=9(x-2)$, so $y=9x-14$. The last option is the normal."}
{"id":"ma-032","s":"maths","t":"p1-12","lv":3,"q":"For $f(x)=x^3-6x^2+9x+1$, which statement about the stationary points is correct?","o":["$(1,5)$ is a minimum and $(3,1)$ is a maximum","$(1,5)$ is a maximum and $(3,1)$ is a minimum","$(1,1)$ is a maximum and $(3,5)$ is a minimum","$(1,5)$ is a maximum and $(3,5)$ is a minimum"],"a":1,"x":"$f'(x)=3(x-1)(x-3)$, so $x=1,3$. $f''(x)=6x-12$: negative at $x=1$ (max), positive at $x=3$ (min). $f(1)=5$ and $f(3)=1$."}
{"id":"ma-033","s":"maths","t":"p1-13","lv":1,"q":"Find $\\int(6x^2+4x^{-3})\\,dx$.","o":["$2x^3+2x^{-2}+c$","$2x^3-2x^{-2}+c$","$2x^3-4x^{-2}+c$","$12x-12x^{-4}+c$"],"a":1,"x":"Add 1 to each power and divide by the new power. $6x^2\\to2x^3$. $4x^{-3}\\to\\frac{4x^{-2}}{-2}=-2x^{-2}$. The last option is differentiation."}
{"id":"ma-034","s":"maths","t":"p1-13","lv":2,"q":"Evaluate $\\int_1^4 3\\sqrt{x}\\,dx$.","o":["$14$","$16$","$21$","$\\frac{21}{2}$"],"a":0,"x":"$\\int3x^{1/2}dx=2x^{3/2}$. Then $2(4)^{3/2}-2(1)^{3/2}=16-2=14$. Subtract the value at the lower limit."}
{"id":"ma-035","s":"maths","t":"p1-13","lv":3,"q":"Find the area of the finite region between the curve $y=x^2-5x+4$ and the $x$-axis.","o":["$-\\frac92$","$\\frac83$","$\\frac92$","$9$"],"a":2,"x":"The curve cuts the axis at $x=1$ and $4$ and lies below it between them. $\\int_1^4=-\\frac92$, and area must be positive, so $\\frac92$."}
{"id":"ma-036","s":"maths","t":"p1-14","lv":1,"q":"Write $2\\log 3+\\log 5$ as a single logarithm.","o":["$\\log 11$","$\\log 30$","$\\log 75$","$\\log 45$"],"a":3,"x":"$2\\log3=\\log9$. Adding logs means multiplying: $\\log9+\\log5=\\log45$."}
{"id":"ma-037","s":"maths","t":"p1-14","lv":2,"q":"Solve $3^x=20$. Give $x$ to 3 significant figures.","o":["$2.73$","$6.67$","$0.367$","$1.30$"],"a":0,"x":"Take logs: $x\\log3=\\log20$, so $x=\\frac{\\log20}{\\log3}=2.73$. The $0.367$ comes from dividing the wrong way round."}
{"id":"ma-038","s":"maths","t":"p1-14","lv":3,"q":"Solve $\\log_2x+\\log_2(x-2)=3$.","o":["$x=4$ or $x=-2$","$x=-2$","$x=5$","$x=4$"],"a":3,"x":"Combine: $\\log_2x(x-2)=3$ so $x^2-2x=8$, giving $x=4$ or $-2$. But $\\log_2$ of a negative number is undefined, so reject $-2$."}
{"id":"ma-039","s":"maths","t":"s1-01","lv":2,"q":"A school has 400 Year 12 and 600 Year 13 students. A stratified sample of 50 students is taken. How many should come from Year 12?","o":["$25$","$30$","$40$","$20$"],"a":3,"x":"Stratified means proportional: $\\frac{400}{1000}\\times50=20$. Year 13 gets the other 30."}
{"id":"ma-040","s":"maths","t":"s1-01","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is an example of opportunity sampling?","o":["Asking the first 30 students who walk into the canteen","Choosing every 10th name from the school register","Giving each student a number and using a random number generator","Randomly choosing from each year group in proportion to its size"],"a":0,"x":"Opportunity sampling uses whoever is available and convenient. The others are systematic, simple random and stratified sampling."}
{"id":"ma-042","s":"maths","t":"s1-02","lv":2,"q":"For 10 values, $\\sum x=60$ and $\\sum x^2=400$. Find the standard deviation.","o":["$4$","$5.83$","$2$","$6.32$"],"a":2,"x":"Variance $=\\frac{400}{10}-\\left(\\frac{60}{10}\\right)^2=40-36=4$. Standard deviation is the square root: $2$."}
{"id":"ma-043","s":"maths","t":"s1-03","lv":2,"q":"For a data set $Q_1=20$ and $Q_3=32$. A value is an outlier if it is more than $1.5\\times$IQR above $Q_3$ or below $Q_1$. Which value is an outlier?","o":["$49$","$45$","$51$","$3$"],"a":2,"x":"IQR $=12$, so $1.5\\times12=18$. Limits: $20-18=2$ and $32+18=50$. Only $51$ is beyond a limit. $3$ is just inside the lower limit."}
{"id":"ma-044","s":"maths","t":"s1-03","lv":1,"q":"In a histogram, the bar for the class $5\\le x<15$ has frequency density 4. What is the frequency of that class?","o":["$2.5$","$16$","$14$","$40$"],"a":3,"x":"Frequency $=$ frequency density $\\times$ class width $=4\\times10=40$. Don't divide."}
{"id":"ma-045","s":"maths","t":"s1-04","lv":1,"q":"A scatter diagram shows the points lying close to a straight line, with $y$ tending to decrease as $x$ increases. Which description is best?","o":["Weak negative linear correlation","Strong negative linear correlation","Strong positive linear correlation","An increase in $x$ causes $y$ to decrease"],"a":1,"x":"Points close to a straight line show strong linear correlation, and $y$ falling as $x$ rises makes it negative. Correlation alone does not show that a change in one variable causes the other to change."}
{"id":"ma-046","s":"maths","t":"s1-05","lv":1,"q":"$A$ and $B$ are independent events with $P(A)=0.4$ and $P(B)=0.5$. Find $P(A\\cup B)$.","o":["$0.9$","$0.2$","$0.7$","$0.3$"],"a":2,"x":"Independent: $P(A\\cap B)=0.4\\times0.5=0.2$. Then $P(A\\cup B)=0.4+0.5-0.2=0.7$. $0.9$ would be for mutually exclusive events."}
{"id":"ma-047","s":"maths","t":"s1-05","lv":2,"q":"$P(A)=0.5$, $P(B)=0.4$ and $P(A\\cup B)=0.8$. Find the probability that exactly one of the events $A$ and $B$ occurs.","o":["$0.1$","$0.7$","$0.5$","$0.8$"],"a":1,"x":"Overlap: $P(A\\cap B)=0.5+0.4-0.8=0.1$. Exactly one means in $A\\cup B$ but not in both: $0.8-0.1=0.7$. Wrongly assuming independence gives $0.5$."}
{"id":"ma-048","s":"maths","t":"s1-06","lv":2,"q":"$X\\sim B(10,0.3)$. Find $P(X=3)$, to 3 significant figures.","o":["$0.267$","$0.650$","$0.383$","$0.617$"],"a":0,"x":"$P(X=3)=\\binom{10}{3}(0.3)^3(0.7)^7=120\\times0.027\\times0.0824=0.267$. The others are $P(X\\le3)$, $P(X<3)$ and $P(X\\ge3)$."}
{"id":"ma-049","s":"maths","t":"s1-06","lv":1,"q":"A fair dice is rolled 12 times. $X$ is the number of sixes. Which distribution models $X$?","o":["$B(6,\\frac1{12})$","$B(12,\\frac12)$","$B(6,\\frac16)$","$B(12,\\frac16)$"],"a":3,"x":"There are $n=12$ trials and the probability of a six on each is $p=\\frac16$. In $B(n,p)$, $n$ always comes first."}
{"id":"ma-050","s":"maths","t":"s1-07","lv":2,"q":"$X\\sim B(10,p)$. Test $H_0:p=0.5$ against $H_1:p>0.5$ at the 5% level. Given $P(X\\ge8)=0.0547$ and $P(X\\ge9)=0.0107$, what is the critical region?","o":["$X\\ge8$","$X\\ge9$","$X\\ge10$","$X=9$"],"a":1,"x":"The critical region needs probability at most 0.05. $P(X\\ge8)=0.0547$ is too big, but $P(X\\ge9)=0.0107$ is fine. So $X\\ge9$."}
{"id":"ma-051","s":"maths","t":"s1-07","lv":3,"q":"A coin is flipped 20 times and $X$ is the number of heads. Test $H_0:p=0.5$ against $H_1:p\\ne0.5$ at the 5% level. Given $P(X\\le5)=0.0207$ and $P(X\\le6)=0.0577$, find the critical region.","o":["$X\\le6$ or $X\\ge14$","$X\\le5$ or $X\\ge16$","$X\\le4$ or $X\\ge16$","$X\\le5$ or $X\\ge15$"],"a":3,"x":"Two-tailed: each tail must be at most 2.5%. $0.0207<0.025$ but $0.0577$ is too big, so lower tail $X\\le5$. By symmetry the upper tail is $X\\ge20-5=15$."}
{"id":"ma-052","s":"maths","t":"m1-08","lv":1,"q":"A ball is modelled as a particle. What does this assume?","o":["It has no mass","It moves at constant speed","Its size is negligible and its mass is at a single point","No forces act on it"],"a":2,"x":"A particle has mass but its dimensions are ignored, so rotation and size effects are neglected. Forces such as weight still act on it."}
{"id":"ma-053","s":"maths","t":"m1-09","lv":1,"q":"A particle has initial speed $4\\text{ m s}^{-1}$ and constant acceleration $3\\text{ m s}^{-2}$. Find its speed after 5 s.","o":["$15\\text{ m s}^{-1}$","$9\\text{ m s}^{-1}$","$19\\text{ m s}^{-1}$","$23\\text{ m s}^{-1}$"],"a":2,"x":"$v=u+at=4+3\\times5=19$. Don't forget to add the initial speed."}
{"id":"ma-054","s":"maths","t":"m1-09","lv":2,"q":"A car slows uniformly from $20\\text{ m s}^{-1}$ to $8\\text{ m s}^{-1}$ over a distance of 84 m. Find the magnitude of its deceleration.","o":["$2\\text{ m s}^{-2}$","$4\\text{ m s}^{-2}$","$0.86\\text{ m s}^{-2}$","$2.76\\text{ m s}^{-2}$"],"a":0,"x":"$v^2=u^2+2as$: $64=400+168a$, so $a=-2$. The deceleration is $2$. Mixing up a sign gives $2.76$."}
{"id":"ma-055","s":"maths","t":"m1-09","lv":3,"q":"A ball is thrown vertically upwards at $12\\text{ m s}^{-1}$ from a point 4 m above the ground. Take $g=9.8\\text{ m s}^{-2}$. Find the time before it hits the ground, to 3 s.f.","o":["$2.75$ s","$2.45$ s","$2.05$ s","$0.297$ s"],"a":0,"x":"Take up as positive, $s=-4$: $-4=12t-4.9t^2$, so $4.9t^2-12t-4=0$. The positive root is $t=2.75$. The negative root $-0.297$ is rejected."}
{"id":"ma-056","s":"maths","t":"m1-10","lv":1,"q":"A resultant force of 12 N acts on a particle of mass 3 kg. Find its acceleration.","o":["$36\\text{ m s}^{-2}$","$0.25\\text{ m s}^{-2}$","$15\\text{ m s}^{-2}$","$4\\text{ m s}^{-2}$"],"a":3,"x":"$F=ma$, so $a=\\frac Fm=\\frac{12}{3}=4\\text{ m s}^{-2}$."}
{"id":"ma-057","s":"maths","t":"m1-10","lv":2,"q":"A 2 kg particle hangs from a vertical string and accelerates upwards at $1.5\\text{ m s}^{-2}$. Take $g=9.8$. Find the tension.","o":["$16.6$ N","$22.6$ N","$19.6$ N","$3$ N"],"a":1,"x":"Upwards: $T-2g=2\\times1.5$. So $T=19.6+3=22.6$ N. Tension must beat the weight to accelerate it upwards."}
{"id":"ma-058","s":"maths","t":"m1-10","lv":3,"q":"Particles of mass 5 kg and 3 kg hang on either side of a smooth light pulley, connected by a light inextensible string. Released from rest, take $g=9.8$. Find the tension in the string.","o":["$49$ N","$36.75$ N","$29.4$ N","$39.2$ N"],"a":1,"x":"Whole system: $a=\\frac{(5-3)g}{8}=2.45$. For the 3 kg particle: $T-3g=3a$, so $T=29.4+7.35=36.75$ N. It lies between the two weights."}
{"id":"ma-059","s":"maths","t":"m1-11","lv":1,"q":"A particle moves with velocity $v=3t^2-4t$ m s⁻¹. Find its acceleration when $t=2$.","o":["$4\\text{ m s}^{-2}$","$8\\text{ m s}^{-2}$","$20\\text{ m s}^{-2}$","$12\\text{ m s}^{-2}$"],"a":1,"x":"Differentiate velocity: $a=6t-4$. At $t=2$, $a=8$. The value $4$ is the velocity at $t=2$, not the acceleration."}
{"id":"ma-060","s":"maths","t":"m1-11","lv":3,"q":"A particle moves on a straight line with velocity $v=3t^2-12t+9$ m s⁻¹ for $t\\ge0$. Find the total distance travelled in the first 2 seconds.","o":["$6$ m","$2$ m","$4$ m","$10$ m"],"a":0,"x":"$s=t^3-6t^2+9t$. $v=0$ at $t=1$ (turns round). $s(1)=4$, $s(2)=2$. Distance $=4+(4-2)=6$ m. The displacement is only 2 m."}
{"id":"ma-061","s":"maths","t":"p1-01","lv":1,"q":"Evaluate $27^{\\frac{2}{3}}$.","o":["$18$","$3$","$9$","$\\frac{1}{9}$"],"a":2,"x":"Take the cube root first: $27^{\\frac13}=3$. Then square it: $3^2=9$. Multiplying 27 by $\\frac23$ to get 18 is the common slip."}
{"id":"ma-062","s":"maths","t":"p1-01","lv":1,"q":"Which expression is equal to $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}$?","o":["$x^{-2}$","$x^{\\frac{1}{2}}$","$x^{-\\frac{1}{2}}$","$-x^{\\frac{1}{2}}$"],"a":2,"x":"A square root is the power $\\frac12$, and taking a reciprocal makes the power negative. So $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}=x^{-\\frac12}$, not a negative of the root."}
{"id":"ma-064","s":"maths","t":"p1-01","lv":1,"q":"A student writes $(2x+3)^2=4x^2+9$. Which is the correct expansion?","o":["$4x^2+12x+9$","$4x^2+6x+9$","$2x^2+12x+9$","$4x^2+12x+6$"],"a":0,"x":"Squaring a bracket gives three terms: $(2x)^2+2(2x)(3)+3^2=4x^2+12x+9$. The student missed the middle term, which is $2\\times 2x\\times 3$."}
{"id":"ma-065","s":"maths","t":"p1-01","lv":2,"q":"Simplify $\\sqrt{50}-\\sqrt{18}$.","o":["$\\sqrt{32}$","$8\\sqrt{2}$","$2$","$2\\sqrt{2}$"],"a":3,"x":"$\\sqrt{50}=5\\sqrt2$ and $\\sqrt{18}=3\\sqrt2$, so the difference is $2\\sqrt2$. You cannot subtract the numbers under the roots: $\\sqrt{32}=4\\sqrt2$ is wrong."}
{"id":"ma-066","s":"maths","t":"p1-01","lv":2,"q":"Solve $\\frac{8^x}{2^{x+3}}=16$.","o":["$x=\\frac{1}{2}$","$x=\\frac{7}{2}$","$x=\\frac{19}{2}$","$x=7$"],"a":1,"x":"Write everything as powers of 2: $\\frac{2^{3x}}{2^{x+3}}=2^{2x-3}=2^4$. So $2x-3=4$ and $x=\\frac72$. Subtract the indices when dividing."}
{"id":"ma-067","s":"maths","t":"p1-02","lv":1,"q":"Which statement is true about the equation $x^2-4x+7=0$?","o":["It has two real roots, because $b^2-4ac=12$","It has no real roots, because $b^2-4ac=-12$","It has equal roots, because $b^2-4ac=0$","It has two real roots, because $b^2-4ac=44$"],"a":1,"x":"$b^2-4ac=(-4)^2-4(1)(7)=16-28=-12$. A negative discriminant means no real roots. Adding $4ac$ instead of subtracting gives 44, a sign slip."}
{"id":"ma-068","s":"maths","t":"p1-02","lv":1,"q":"Which describes the graph of $y=3+2x-x^2$?","o":["It has a minimum and crosses the $y$-axis at 3","It has a maximum and crosses the $y$-axis at 2","It has a maximum and crosses the $y$-axis at 3","It has a minimum and crosses the $y$-axis at 2"],"a":2,"x":"The $x^2$ coefficient is $-1$, which is negative, so the graph has a maximum. Putting $x=0$ gives $y=3$. The 2 is the coefficient of $x$, not the intercept."}
{"id":"ma-069","s":"maths","t":"p1-02","lv":2,"q":"A quadratic graph crosses the $x$-axis at $-2$ and $5$ and the $y$-axis at $-20$. Which is its equation?","o":["$y=2x^2+6x-20$","$y=x^2-3x-10$","$y=-2x^2+6x+20$","$y=2x^2-6x-20$"],"a":3,"x":"Write $y=a(x+2)(x-5)$. At $x=0$: $a(2)(-5)=-20$, so $a=2$. Expanding gives $2x^2-6x-20$. Leaving out $a$ gives $x^2-3x-10$, which meets the $y$-axis at $-10$."}
{"id":"ma-070","s":"maths","t":"p1-02","lv":3,"q":"The equation $x^2+2kx+(k+6)=0$ has no real roots. Which gives all possible values of $k$?","o":["$k<-2$ or $k>3$","$-2<k<3$","$-3<k<2$","$-2\\le k\\le 3$"],"a":1,"x":"Need $4k^2-4(k+6)<0$, so $k^2-k-6<0$, i.e. $(k-3)(k+2)<0$. A U-shaped graph is negative between its roots: $-2<k<3$. No real roots needs a strict inequality."}
{"id":"ma-071","s":"maths","t":"p1-03","lv":1,"q":"Solve $3-2x<11$.","o":["$x<-4$","$x>4$","$x>-4$","$x<4$"],"a":2,"x":"Subtract 3: $-2x<8$. Dividing both sides by $-2$ reverses the inequality sign, so $x>-4$."}
{"id":"ma-072","s":"maths","t":"p1-03","lv":2,"q":"The line $y=2x-1$ and the curve $y=x^2-2x+3$ are drawn on the same axes. Which statement is correct?","o":["The line crosses the curve at two distinct points","It is a tangent, touching the curve at $(2,3)$","The line does not meet the curve","It is a tangent, touching the curve at $(1,1)$"],"a":1,"x":"Setting $x^2-2x+3=2x-1$ gives $x^2-4x+4=0$, so $(x-2)^2=0$. One repeated root $x=2$ means the line is a tangent, and $y=2(2)-1=3$."}
{"id":"ma-073","s":"maths","t":"p1-03","lv":2,"q":"Solve $2x^2+3x<5$.","o":["$x<-\\frac{5}{2}$ or $x>1$","$-1<x<\\frac{5}{2}$","$x<1$","$-\\frac{5}{2}<x<1$"],"a":3,"x":"Rearrange to $2x^2+3x-5<0$, so $(2x+5)(x-1)<0$. The U-shaped graph is below the axis between its roots, giving $-\\frac52<x<1$."}
{"id":"ma-075","s":"maths","t":"p1-04","lv":1,"q":"Which transformation maps the graph of $y=f(x)$ onto $y=f(2x)$?","o":["A stretch parallel to the $x$-axis, scale factor 2","A stretch parallel to the $y$-axis, scale factor 2","A stretch parallel to the $y$-axis, scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$","A stretch parallel to the $x$-axis, scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$"],"a":3,"x":"Replacing $x$ by $2x$ means each $x$-coordinate is halved, so it is a horizontal stretch with scale factor $\\frac12$. The change inside the bracket acts on $x$ and uses the reciprocal."}
{"id":"ma-076","s":"maths","t":"p1-04","lv":1,"q":"Write down the equations of the asymptotes of $y=\\frac{1}{x-3}+2$.","o":["$x=-3$ and $y=2$","$x=3$ and $y=-2$","$x=2$ and $y=3$","$x=3$ and $y=2$"],"a":3,"x":"$y=\\frac1x$ has asymptotes $x=0$ and $y=0$. Replacing $x$ by $x-3$ moves the graph right 3, and $+2$ moves it up 2, so the asymptotes are $x=3$ and $y=2$."}
{"id":"ma-077","s":"maths","t":"p1-04","lv":1,"q":"Which transformation maps the graph of $y=f(x)$ onto $y=f(-x)$?","o":["Reflection in the $y$-axis","Reflection in the $x$-axis","Translation by $\\begin{pmatrix}-1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}$","Rotation of $180^\\circ$ about the origin"],"a":0,"x":"Changing $x$ to $-x$ swaps left and right, so the graph is reflected in the $y$-axis. Reflection in the $x$-axis would give $y=-f(x)$."}
{"id":"ma-078","s":"maths","t":"p1-04","lv":2,"q":"The point $(3,-2)$ lies on $y=f(x)$. Which point lies on $y=f(x+1)-4$?","o":["$(2,-6)$","$(4,-6)$","$(2,2)$","$(4,2)$"],"a":0,"x":"$f(x+1)$ shifts the graph 1 to the left, giving $(2,-2)$. Then $-4$ shifts it 4 down, giving $(2,-6)$. Moving right instead of left is the usual slip."}
{"id":"ma-079","s":"maths","t":"p1-04","lv":2,"q":"Which describes where the curve $y=(x-1)^2(x+2)$ meets the axes?","o":["Crosses at $x=1$, touches at $x=-2$, $y$-intercept 2","Touches at $x=1$, crosses at $x=-2$, $y$-intercept 2","Touches at $x=1$, crosses at $x=-2$, $y$-intercept $-2$","Touches at $x=1$, crosses at $x=-2$, $y$-intercept 4"],"a":1,"x":"A repeated factor $(x-1)^2$ means the curve touches the axis at 1; the single factor means it crosses at $-2$. At $x=0$, $y=(-1)^2(2)=2$."}
{"id":"ma-080","s":"maths","t":"p1-04","lv":3,"q":"The curve $y=x^2-4x+3$ is reflected in the $x$-axis and then translated by $\\begin{pmatrix}2\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}$. Find the equation of the new curve.","o":["$y=-x^2+1$","$y=x^2-8x+15$","$y=-x^2+8x-15$","$y=-x^2+4x-5$"],"a":2,"x":"Reflection gives $y=-f(x)$. Moving right 2 replaces $x$ by $x-2$: $y=-[(x-2)^2-4(x-2)+3]=-x^2+8x-15$. Replacing $x$ by $x+2$ instead gives $-x^2+1$."}
{"id":"ma-081","s":"maths","t":"p1-05","lv":1,"q":"Which line is parallel to $y=3x-2$?","o":["$x+3y=1$","$3y=x+4$","$6x-2y+5=0$","$y=-3x+1$"],"a":2,"x":"Parallel lines have the same gradient, 3. Rearranging $6x-2y+5=0$ gives $y=3x+\\frac52$. The other lines have gradients $-\\frac13$, $\\frac13$ and $-3$."}
{"id":"ma-082","s":"maths","t":"p1-05","lv":1,"q":"A line has equation $4x-3y+9=0$. What is the gradient of a line perpendicular to it?","o":["$\\frac{3}{4}$","$-\\frac{4}{3}$","$-\\frac{3}{4}$","$\\frac{4}{3}$"],"a":2,"x":"Rearrange: $y=\\frac43x+3$, so its gradient is $\\frac43$. A perpendicular gradient is the negative reciprocal, $-\\frac34$."}
{"id":"ma-083","s":"maths","t":"p1-05","lv":2,"q":"Find the equation of the line through $(-1,4)$ and $(3,-2)$ in the form $ax+by+c=0$.","o":["$3x-2y+11=0$","$2x+3y-10=0$","$3x+2y+5=0$","$3x+2y-5=0$"],"a":3,"x":"Gradient $=\\frac{-2-4}{3-(-1)}=-\\frac32$. Then $y-4=-\\frac32(x+1)$, which rearranges to $3x+2y-5=0$. Check by substituting the other point."}
{"id":"ma-084","s":"maths","t":"p1-05","lv":3,"q":"$A$ is $(1,2)$ and $B$ is $(5,0)$. The perpendicular bisector of $AB$ meets the $y$-axis at $C$. Find the area of triangle $ABC$.","o":["30","15","5","7.5"],"a":1,"x":"Midpoint $M$ is $(3,1)$ and $AB$ has gradient $-\\frac12$, so the bisector is $y=2x-5$ and $C=(0,-5)$. $CM$ is perpendicular to $AB$, so area $=\\frac12\\times2\\sqrt5\\times3\\sqrt5=15$. Forgetting the $\\frac12$ gives 30."}
{"id":"ma-085","s":"maths","t":"s1-01","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is a census?","o":["Measuring the height of every student in a school","Measuring the heights of 100 students chosen at random from the school","Measuring the heights of the first 20 students to arrive at school","Measuring the heights of 10 students from each year group"],"a":0,"x":"A census observes every member of the population. All the other methods use only part of the population, so they are samples."}
{"id":"ma-086","s":"maths","t":"s1-01","lv":1,"q":"Which of these variables is continuous?","o":["The number of siblings a student has","A student's shoe size","The time taken to run 100 m","The number of goals scored in a match"],"a":2,"x":"Continuous data can take any value in a range, such as a time. The others take separate values only (counts or shoe sizes), so they are discrete."}
{"id":"ma-087","s":"maths","t":"s1-01","lv":1,"q":"What is a sampling frame?","o":["The sample that is finally selected from the population","A list of all members of the population from which the sample is chosen","The proportion of the population included in the sample","The set of questions asked in a survey"],"a":1,"x":"A sampling frame is the list of sampling units in the population that the sample is drawn from. A good frame is complete and has no repeats."}
{"id":"ma-088","s":"maths","t":"s1-01","lv":2,"q":"A systematic sample of 40 is taken from items numbered 1 to 600. The first item, chosen at random, is item 7. Which are the next three items selected?","o":["$21,\\ 35,\\ 49$","$22,\\ 37,\\ 52$","$47,\\ 87,\\ 127$","$14,\\ 21,\\ 28$"],"a":1,"x":"The interval is $600\\div 40=15$. Starting at 7, the next items are $7+15=22$, then $37$, then $52$. Using 40 or 7 as the interval is the common error."}
{"id":"ma-089","s":"maths","t":"s1-01","lv":2,"q":"Which statement about quota sampling is correct?","o":["A full sampling frame is needed and the selection is random","Every member of the population has an equal chance of being chosen","No sampling frame is needed, but it is not random, so bias is possible","It is a census of each group in the population"],"a":2,"x":"In quota sampling an interviewer fills quotas for each group, without a sampling frame. It is quick and cheap, but the choice of individuals is not random, so it can be biased."}
{"id":"ma-090","s":"maths","t":"s1-01","lv":2,"q":"A stratified sample of 45 is taken from the 750 students in a school, by year group, with each year in exact proportion. 18 of the sample are in Year 12. How many Year 12 students are at the school?","o":["300","450","270","120"],"a":0,"x":"Stratified sampling is proportional to group size, so $\\frac{18}{45}=\\frac{n}{750}$. Then $n=750\\times\\frac{18}{45}=300$."}
{"id":"ma-091","s":"maths","t":"s1-02","lv":1,"q":"Which measure of spread is least affected by one extremely large value in a data set?","o":["Range","Interquartile range","Standard deviation","Variance"],"a":1,"x":"The range, variance and standard deviation all use the extreme value, so it changes them a lot. The interquartile range uses only the middle half of the data."}
{"id":"ma-092","s":"maths","t":"s1-02","lv":2,"q":"A data set has mean 10 and standard deviation 2. Every value is multiplied by 3, then 4 is subtracted. What are the new mean and standard deviation?","o":["Mean 26, standard deviation 6","Mean 26, standard deviation 2","Mean 18, standard deviation 6","Mean 18, standard deviation 2"],"a":0,"x":"Mean: $3(10)-4=26$. Multiplying scales the standard deviation to $3\\times2=6$, but subtracting 4 only shifts the data, so it does not change the spread."}
{"id":"ma-093","s":"maths","t":"s1-02","lv":2,"q":"A data set of 8 values has $\\sum(x-\\bar{x})^2=72$. What is the standard deviation $\\sigma$?","o":["$9$","$6\\sqrt{2}$","$3$","$\\frac{9}{8}$"],"a":2,"x":"Variance $=\\frac{72}{8}=9$. The standard deviation is the square root of the variance, so $\\sigma=3$. The value 9 is the variance, not the standard deviation."}
{"id":"ma-094","s":"maths","t":"s1-02","lv":3,"q":"Set $A$ has 5 values with mean 8 and standard deviation 2. Set $B$ has 5 values with mean 12 and standard deviation 2. What is the standard deviation of all 10 values?","o":["$2\\sqrt{2}$","$2$","$4$","$8$"],"a":0,"x":"Combined mean is 10. $\\sum x^2=5(4+64)+5(4+144)=1080$, so variance $=\\frac{1080}{10}-10^2=8$ and $\\sigma=2\\sqrt2$. The sets are centred apart, so it is not 2."}
{"id":"ma-095","s":"maths","t":"m1-08","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is a vector quantity?","o":["Distance","Speed","Displacement","Mass"],"a":2,"x":"A vector has both magnitude and direction. Displacement does. Distance, speed and mass have size only, so they are scalars."}
{"id":"ma-096","s":"maths","t":"m1-08","lv":1,"q":"A rope is modelled as a light string. What does 'light' mean here?","o":["It cannot stretch","It is perfectly smooth","It has no thickness","Its mass is negligible"],"a":3,"x":"'Light' means the mass of the string can be ignored. 'Inextensible' means it cannot stretch, and 'smooth' means there is no friction."}
{"id":"ma-097","s":"maths","t":"m1-08","lv":1,"q":"Which unit is equivalent to a newton?","o":["$\\text{kg m s}^{-1}$","$\\text{kg m}^2\\text{ s}^{-2}$","$\\text{kg s}^{-2}$","$\\text{kg m s}^{-2}$"],"a":3,"x":"Since $F=ma$, a newton is a kilogram multiplied by a metre per second squared: $\\text{kg m s}^{-2}$."}
{"id":"ma-098","s":"maths","t":"m1-08","lv":2,"q":"Two particles are joined by a string passing over a fixed pulley. Which assumption ensures the particles always have the same speed?","o":["The string is inextensible","The string is light","The pulley is smooth","The particles have equal mass"],"a":0,"x":"If the string cannot stretch, one particle moves down exactly as far as the other moves up, so their speeds are always equal. 'Light' and 'smooth' make the tension the same on both sides; they do not make the speeds equal."}
{"id":"ma-099","s":"maths","t":"m2-04","lv":2,"q":"A plank is modelled as a rod. Which pair of assumptions means its weight acts at its midpoint and it does not bend?","o":["Light and rigid","Uniform and rigid","Smooth and uniform","Inextensible and light"],"a":1,"x":"Uniform means the mass is spread evenly, so the weight acts at the midpoint. Rigid means it does not bend. A light rod would have no weight to place."}
{"id":"ma-100","s":"maths","t":"m1-08","lv":2,"q":"A force of 3.6 kN acts on a particle of mass 800 g. Find its acceleration, in $\\text{m s}^{-2}$.","o":["$4500$","$4.5$","$0.0045$","$45$"],"a":0,"x":"Convert to SI units first: $3.6\\text{ kN}=3600\\text{ N}$ and $800\\text{ g}=0.8\\text{ kg}$. Then $a=\\frac{3600}{0.8}=4500$. Converting only one unit gives 4.5; converting neither gives 0.0045."}
{"id":"ma-101","s":"maths","t":"m1-09","lv":1,"q":"A ball is thrown vertically upwards. Ignoring air resistance, which statement is true at its highest point?","o":["Its velocity is zero and its acceleration is zero","Its velocity is $9.8\\text{ m s}^{-1}$ and its acceleration is zero","Its velocity is zero and its acceleration is $9.8\\text{ m s}^{-2}$ upwards","Its velocity is zero and its acceleration is $9.8\\text{ m s}^{-2}$ downwards"],"a":3,"x":"Gravity acts throughout the flight, so the acceleration is $9.8\\text{ m s}^{-2}$ downwards at every point. Only the velocity is momentarily zero at the top."}
{"id":"ma-102","s":"maths","t":"m1-09","lv":2,"q":"A stone is thrown upwards at $10\\text{ m s}^{-1}$. A student uses $0=10t+4.9t^2$ to find when it returns to its start and gets a negative time. What is the error?","o":["The displacement should be 10 m, not 0","Taking upwards as positive, the acceleration should be $-9.8$","The value of $g$ should be 10","$v=u+at$ should be used instead of $s=ut+\\frac{1}{2}at^2$"],"a":1,"x":"With upwards positive, $u=10$ but $a=-9.8$. Then $0=10t-4.9t^2$ gives $t=\\frac{10}{4.9}\\approx2.04$ s. Using $+9.8$ ignores that gravity acts against the motion."}
{"id":"ma-103","s":"maths","t":"m1-09","lv":2,"q":"Ignoring air resistance, a ball dropped from rest from height $h$ takes time $t$ to reach the ground. How long does it take when dropped from height $4h$?","o":["$4t$","$16t$","$\\sqrt{2}\\,t$","$2t$"],"a":3,"x":"From rest, $s=\\frac12gt^2$, so $t=\\sqrt{\\frac{2s}{g}}$ and $t$ is proportional to $\\sqrt{s}$. Four times the height gives $\\sqrt4=2$ times the time."}
{"id":"ma-104","s":"maths","t":"m1-09","lv":3,"q":"A particle moves in a straight line with initial velocity $6\\text{ m s}^{-1}$ and constant acceleration $-2\\text{ m s}^{-2}$. Find the total distance it travels in the first 4 seconds, in metres.","o":["$10$","$8$","$9$","$12$"],"a":0,"x":"From $v=u+at$ it comes to rest at $t=3$, having gone $\\frac{6+0}{2}\\times3=9$ m. In the last second it goes back $\\frac12\\times2\\times1^2=1$ m. Total distance 10 m; the displacement is only 8 m."}
